    <md:title>2.11.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>80a43428-c137-4ed2-b09e-a60a6049ad0f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<para>For 1 - 3, determine whether each ordered pair is a solution to the inequality \(y &gt; x − 1\).</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="4f57fb23-4a13-46c7-bade-4751d590d15b"><problem><para>
      <para> \((0,1)\) </para>
      </para></problem><solution><para>Yes</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is Yes. Substitute the ordered pair \((0, 1)\) into the inequality \(y &gt; x − 1\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution.
        \(1 &gt; 0 − 1\) \(1 &gt; − 1\) 1 is greater than -1. Yes, it is a solution because the inequality is true.</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><!--retries set to 2 in this section-->
<!--BEGIN PRACTICE PROBLEM MULTIPLE CHOICE -->
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="ded25a4b-2d81-4d4d-9bff-397f05af0ea0"><problem><para>
      <para>Which inequality represents the graph?</para>
      <figure><media alt="graph of y &lt; 3x − 4"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8f554c15c06d4fbb6743789abf840d7e817b0442"/></media></figure></para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(y\;\leq\;3x\;-\;4\)</para><para>\(y &lt; 3x − 4\)</para><para>\(y &gt; 3x − 4\)</para><para>\(y\;\geq\;3x\;-\;4\)</para></problem><solution><para>\(y &lt; 3x − 4\)</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is \(y &lt; 3x − 4\). The graph has a dotted line, so any answers that have \(\geq\) or\(\leq\) can be eliminated. 
        The two remaining options are \(y&gt;3x−4\) and \(y&gt;3x−4\).
        If we set \(x\) to 0, we can see that the \(y\)-coordinate is -4. Look at the shading in the graph to determine if y&gt;-4 or \(y&gt;-4\). The graph is shaded to the right and down so \(y&gt;-4\). Therefore the equation \(y&gt;3x−4\) is the correct answer.</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><!--END PRACTICE PROBLEM MULTIPLE CHOICE -->

<para>For questions 10 and 11, use this graph of the equation \(2y−x=1\) as a guide to answer the questions.</para>
<figure><media alt="Graph of a line."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9d4d8b641a0b7f9aa831396e126584e15641988e"/></media></figure><!--Retries set to 1 in this section-->
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="7f152e76-fb43-4be0-8bfa-4843d43903b0"><problem><para>
      <para>Is the point \((0, \frac12)\) a solution to the equation?</para>
      </para></problem><solution><para>Yes</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is Yes. Substitute the ordered pair \((0, \frac{1}{2})\) into the equation \(2y−x=1\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution. 
        \(2(\frac{1}{2})−0=1\)\(1-0=1\)\(1=1\) 1 equals 1. Yes, it is a solution because the equation is true.
      </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><!--Retries set tp 1 this section-->
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="04793c5c-6570-44f1-9a26-ee459ae51b57"><problem><para>
      <para>Consider the linear equation \(2y − 3x = 5\). The pair \((-1,1)\) is a solution to the equation. Which of
          the following is also a solution to the equation?</para>
      </para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\((1, 5)\)</para><para>\((3, 7)\)</para><para>\((5, 11)\)</para><para>\((-3, -1)\)</para></problem><solution><para>\((3, 7)\)</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is (3, 7).
      </para>
      <para>Substitute the ordered pair \((3, 7)\) into the equation \(2y−3x=5\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution. 
        \(2(7)−3(3)=5\)
        \(14-9=5\)
        \(5=5\)
        5 equals 5. The ordered pair (3, 7) is a solution because the equation is true.</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
